Z||000006||DJRice||02-08-2000||09:39 AM||||The plugs in a v-6 Fiero arent any harder to change than in any other FWD v-6 Car on the market today.  Get yourself a 1/4" drive socket set and your in there.  People see a car with the engine in the back and freak out when it comes to working on it.  Its no harder than any other car.  <P><BR>Besides that, if everything was <B>easy</B>, what <B>fun</B> would it be?||||reg||1
Z||000007||mrfixit58||02-08-2000||10:07 AM||||It has been a long time since I've change the plug on a V6 Fiero but I still have my "modofied" socket. I used a sparkplug socket that has the hexigon shape on the end. I had to grind a little metal off the socket end to fit into the head. Once the socket was installed over the plug, I used a 3/4(?) wrench to loosen the plug. <P>If memory serves me correctly I only had to do this with one plug and it was the one closes to the driver's seat. I believe, in the 80,000 miles I put on my 85 GT, I changed the plugs about 3-4 times and the wires, cap and rotor button once.<P>Other than the A/C compressor and the electronic volume buttons on the radio, I never had any problems with the car. maybe that's another reason I bought the 87 I have now. That and the the "seat-of-the-pants" thrill of driving it!<P>Roy L.||||reg||3
